{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16888 - PROTEIN:5752",
  "verbalization": "Theasaponin E1 appears to have a shared target in RPTEC through multiple indirect pathways, all involving the 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline compound. This compound is identified as a target of both Theasaponin E1 and K-252a, which in turn targets RPTEC. The presence of multiple overlapping targets suggests a potential mechanism where Theasaponin E1 may act through a common pathway, possibly by modulating the activity of the phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta, which is a key component in the signaling cascade leading to RPTEC activation. The consistent involvement of 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline across different pathways reinforces the idea of a shared molecular mechanism, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
